   "source": [
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "- Respondents reporting prior AI coursework have a mean AI-literacy score of 3.556, compared with 3.601 among respondents without prior coursework.\n",
    "- The difference is small and not statistically significant (mean difference = -0.045, Welch p = 0.423; Cohen's d = -0.108).\n",
    "- Prior-course prevalence is strongly imbalanced across source cohorts, including 23/23 Yes responses in legacy 1204. The pooled test is therefore descriptive and should not be interpreted as the causal effect of taking an AI course."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "summary-heading",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Results summary\n",
    "\n",
    "- The pooled dataset contains 232 unique respondents: 141 from the original exports and 91 from the 2026 exports.\n",
    "- Overall AI-literacy reliability is low (Cronbach's α = 0.50), so the composite should be interpreted cautiously.\n",
    "- SES has a small positive association with overall AI literacy (standardized β = 0.209, HC3 p = 0.001, 95% CI [0.087, 0.330]), explaining 4.4% of the variance. This observational result is not a causal estimate.\n",
    "- The theory-guided two-factor AI solution explains 29.2% of item variance (KMO = 0.658); three eigenvalues exceed 1, so the two-factor structure remains exploratory. The SES two-factor solution explains 40.0% (KMO = 0.728).\n",
    "- None of the 20 bootstrapped indirect effects survives FDR correction (smallest adjusted p = 0.347) or Holm correction (smallest adjusted p = 0.573).\n",
    "- Ten partial-correlation edges survive FDR correction; three survive Holm correction. The Holm-robust edges are conceptual exposure–learning ecology (partial r = 0.362), language load–AI understanding (r = -0.324), and epistemic stance–AI understanding (r = 0.253).\n",
    "- Adding mediators significantly improves both hierarchical models (ΔR² = 0.183 for AI understanding and 0.127 for AI confidence). Interaction blocks also improve fit (ΔR² = 0.032 and 0.034), although no individual interaction coefficient survives the within-model FDR correction.\n",
    "- Respondents with and without prior AI coursework have similar overall AI-literacy scores (3.556 vs 3.601; Welch p = 0.423; Cohen's d = -0.108). This comparison is descriptive and strongly confounded with source cohort."
   ]
  }
